{"id": "EUVD-2026-80465", "enisaUuid": "4ee347bd-cc16-35d8-9b2d-9c3fa784ec1d", "description": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nscsi: qla2xxx: Zero SFP DMA buffer in FRU/I2C bsg handlers\n\nThe FRU and I2C bsg handlers stage their transfer in a DMA_POOL_SIZE\n(256-byte) bounce buffer obtained from dma_pool_alloc(), which does not\nzero the allocation. They initialize only a few leading bytes before\nhanding the buffer to qla2x00_write_sfp().\n\nqla2x00_write_sfp() can override the transfer length with a user-supplied\nvalue:\n\n\tif (len == 1)\n\t\topt |= BIT_0;\n\tif (opt & BIT_0)\n\t\tlen = *sfp;\n\n*sfp is the first byte of the (user-controlled) payload, so len can grow\nup to 255. The device then DMA-reads len bytes from the 256-byte pool\nbuffer. Since only a small prefix was written\n(e.g. MAX_FRU_SIZE == 36 bytes for a FRU version, one byte for a FRU\nstatus register), the hardware reads past the initialized region and\nwrites up to ~219 bytes of stale DMA-pool heap memory to the device\nflash.\n\nAllocate the buffer with dma_pool_zalloc() in all five FRU/I2C handlers\nso any bytes beyond the initialized data are zero rather than stale heap\ncontents.", "datePublished": "2026-09-16T10:31:35", "dateUpdated": "2026-10-19T08:53:58", "baseScore": 0.0, "references": ["https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/a476377a66897549dd49bee319f4df66623417b7", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/09703bc7c0be3a7a155b0ff5f21f6765ba3f519c", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/97c45c75f5cdec96b1a4fba8b1d55d0dd01af1e8", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/84bde5ce4038d9ad811e5c994305bbfcbd7a9f79", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/581590f560b74399151b3cbc88574424c2f3d2dc", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/b157256c28086c434afd70cc78bf9b4d8caf1276", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/a5501c42256235523c4dddf799f032dfbf4f4c77", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/b47d4a1547d9ef21b2e9d1a739fe2204d4be05dc"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-89865", "GHSA-rqfh-jqm9-82qm"], "assigner": "Linux", "epss": 0.21, "enisaIdProduct": [{"id": "1009a9a0-3c98-3a0a-86e5-d7399d32475d", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "697a4bc69159c3396035b0506ffa55c4b2d0b1f4 <97c45c75f5cdec96b1a4fba8b1d55d0dd01af1e8"}, {"id": "15989be3-8a94-3045-980a-2f619c837500", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "697a4bc69159c3396035b0506ffa55c4b2d0b1f4 <581590f560b74399151b3cbc88574424c2f3d2dc"}, {"id": "21ef1980-1706-3401-b06d-bed05ae19eeb", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.18.51"}, {"id": "32a30aa6-51f3-3a27-aaa1-a3caf22daaf6", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 7.3"}, {"id": "378e6449-6cfd-3d34-8bd3-72268a4813e7", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.12.110"}, {"id": "423b95ed-67d6-322d-ad01-7c840dd3f07d", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 0"}, {"id": "53880b42-755a-3b21-8b57-fdcce3408e93", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "3.2"}, {"id": "59c73dbe-0f88-32f5-8e6a-b796c4c0ae6e", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.1.188"}, {"id": "7ebc281a-8bce-3ee1-8a43-ed9db52b892e", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "697a4bc69159c3396035b0506ffa55c4b2d0b1f4 <a476377a66897549dd49bee319f4df66623417b7"}, {"id": "86da8df7-5579-3cb7-8c83-48c5e01f48bf", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "697a4bc69159c3396035b0506ffa55c4b2d0b1f4 <84bde5ce4038d9ad811e5c994305bbfcbd7a9f79"}, {"id": "8e1cb61e-211b-30cf-8437-8a8d8ffc1413", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "697a4bc69159c3396035b0506ffa55c4b2d0b1f4 <b47d4a1547d9ef21b2e9d1a739fe2204d4be05dc"}, {"id": "9a73746b-3eb5-3829-b2ea-fffc58db85b0", "pro